adiness:\n","Pedagogical    0.503850\n","Digital        0.420913\n","Training       0.337169\n","Resource       0.331726\n","Name: overall_readiness, dtype: float64\n","\n","Correlation analysis complete. I now have a clear understanding of the primary drivers of readiness.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Examining variation in individual items versus overall_readiness\n","print(\"COMPARING VARIATION: ITEMS VS. OVERALL READINESS\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Calculating variance for the overall_readiness target variable\n","readiness_var = df_validated['overall_readiness'].var()\n","print(f\"Variance of overall_readiness: {readiness_var:.4f}\")\n","\n","# Grouping all construct items for a comprehensive variance check\n","all_items = pedagogical_items + digital_items + resource_items + training_items\n","item_variances = {}\n","\n","# Calculating variance for each individual survey item\n","print(\"\\nVariance of individual items:\")\n","for item in all_items:\n","    var = df_validated[item].var()\n","    item_variances[item] = var\n","    print(f\"  {item}: {var:.4f}\")\n","\n","# Generating summary statistics for item-level variances\n","var_series = pd.Series(item_variances)\n","print(f\"\\nSummary of Item Variances:\")\n","print(f\"  Mean item variance: {var_series.mean():.4f}\")\n","print(f\"  Min item variance: {var_series.min():.4f}\")\n","print(f\"  Max item variance: {var_series.max():.4f}\")\n","print(f\"  Percentage of items with higher variance than target: {(var_series > readiness_var).mean()*100:.1f}%\")\n","\n","# Visualizing the variance comparison\n","plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\n","\n","# Plotting individual item variances against the target baseline\n","plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\n","plt.bar(range(len(var_series)), var_series.values)\n","plt.axhline(y=readiness_var, color='r', linestyle='--', label=f'Overall Readiness baseline: {readiness_var:.4f}')\n","plt.xlabel('Items')\n","plt.ylabel('Variance')\n","plt.title('Variance of Individual Items')\n","plt.legend()\n","\n","# Plotting the distribution of variances\n","plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\n","plt.hist(var_series.values, bins=20, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\n","plt.axvline(x=readiness_var, color='r', linestyle='--', label='Overall Readiness Baseline')\n","plt.xlabel('Variance')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","plt.title('Distribution of Item Variances')\n","plt.legend()\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","print(\"\\nVariability analysis complete. I have identified the distribution of information across the items.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"O5Z3q5yZWGrl","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331394783,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":695,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"6aa4a91d-8003-46e4-b139-a3bf9f640e9b"},"execution_count":40,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["COMPARING VARIATION: ITEMS VS. OVERALL READINESS\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Variance of overall_readiness: 0.2527\n","\n","Variance of individual items:\n","  conf_B1: 0.7618\n","  conf_B2: 0.7976\n","  conf_B3: 0.7648\n","  conf_B4: 0.8205\n","  conf_B5: 0.7776\n","  conf_B6: 0.7627\n","  conf_B7: 0.7886\n","  digi_C1a: 1.0225\n","  digi_C1b: 1.0115\n","  digi_C1c: 1.0175\n","  digi_C1d: 1.0002\n","  digi_C2a: 0.6698\n","  digi_C2b: 0.6461\n","  digi_C2c: 0.6700\n","  digi_C2d: 0.6611\n","  digi_C2e: 0.6914\n","  res_D1: 0.9407\n","  res_D2: 0.9680\n","  res_D3: 0.9682\n","  res_D4: 0.9308\n","  res_D5: 0.9367\n","  res_D6: 0.9305\n","  train_E2: 1.4618\n","  train_E3: 1.4234\n","  train_E4: 1.3963\n","  train_E5: 1.3983\n","  train_E6: 1.4513\n","  train_E7: 1.4501\n","\n","Summary of Item Variances:\n","  Mean item variance: 0.9686\n","  Min item variance: 0.6461\n","  Max item variance: 1.4618\n","  Percentage of items with higher variance than target: 100.0%\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1200x500 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Variability analysis complete. I have identified the distribution of information across the items.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["From the variance analysis, it is evident that a significant discrepancy exists between the individual survey items and the overall_readiness score. While the target variable has a restricted variance of 0.2527, we can see that the individual items show a mean variance of 0.9686—nearly four times higher. In fact, every single survey item demonstrates substantially more variation than the overall readiness score. The training-related items show the highest levels of variation, while the digital competency items show the least, yet even the lowest-variance item is still 2.5 times more varied than the target variable.\n","\n","We can see from this comparison a critical limitation: the overall_readiness score is severely restricted. Because a vast majority of teachers rated themselves a \"3,\" an artificial ceiling has been created that masks the nuanced differences in teacher capability. Using this as the primary target variable would likely result in an underperforming model, as there is insufficient variation for the machine learning algorithms to learn meaningful patterns.\n","\n","Consequently, the decision has been made to pivot the methodology. Rather than predicting the restricted overall_readiness score, the next step involves developing a custom Teacher Readiness Index (TRI). This index will be synthesized from the 28 validated items, which capture a far richer and more accurate picture of the teachers' actual readiness. We will now determine the optimal method for this construction—whether through a simple mean, a weighted index based on construct importance, or the use of factor scores—to ensure the resulting index is both statistically robust and practically useful for policy recommendations.\n","\n","Finding: overall_readiness has insufficient variance | Action: Develop custom TRI from individual items"],"metadata":{"id":"QHN1ook1WQYZ"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Checking Item-Total Correlations and Internal Consistency\n","print(\"ITEM CORRELATIONS AND INTERNAL CONSISTENCY\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Calculating the mean of all 28 items to create a preliminary composite score\n","df_validated['items_composite'] = df_validated[all_items].mean(axis=1)\n","\n","# Evaluating item correlations with the original overall_readiness for comparison\n","print(\"\\nCorrelations with Overall Readiness (Reference):\")\n","readiness_corrs = {}\n","for item in all_items:\n","    corr = df_validated[item].corr(df_validated['overall_readiness'])\n","    readiness_corrs[item] = corr\n","\n","readiness_corr_series = pd.Series(readiness_corrs)\n","print(f\"Mean correlation with overall_readiness: {readiness_corr_series.abs().mean():.4f}\")\n","print(f\"Range: {readiness_corr_series.abs().min():.4f} to {readiness_corr_series.abs().max():.4f}\")\n","\n","# Evaluating item correlations with the new composite (potential TRI)\n","print(\"\\nCorrelations with Items Composite (Potential TRI):\")\n","composite_corrs = {}\n","for item in all_items:\n","    corr = df_validated[item].corr(df_validated['items_composite'])\n","    composite_corrs[item] = corr\n","\n","composite_corr_series = pd.Series(composite_corrs)\n","print(f\"Mean correlation with composite: {composite_corr_series.abs().mean():.4f}\")\n","print(f\"Range: {composite_corr_series.abs().min():.4f} to {composite_corr_series.abs().max():.4f}\")\n","\n","# Identifying the most and least influential items for the composite\n","print(\"\\nTop 5 Items by Composite Correlation:\")\n","print(composite_corr_series.sort_values(ascending=False).head(5))\n","\n","print(\"\\nBottom 5 Items by Composite Correlation:\")\n","print(composite_corr_series.sort_values(ascending=True).head(5))\n","\n","# Visualizing the consistency and correlation strengths\n","plt.figure(figsize=(15, 5))\n","\n","# Scatter plot comparing item relationships across both target variables\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)\n","plt.scatter(readiness_corr_series.abs(), composite_corr_series.abs(), alpha=0.5)\n","plt.xlabel('Correlation with Overall Readiness (abs)')\n","plt.ylabel('Correlation with Composite (abs)')\n","plt.title('Readiness vs. Composite Comparison')\n","# Diagonal reference line\n","max_val = max(readiness_corr_series.abs().max(), composite_corr_series.abs().max())\n","plt.plot([0, max_val], [0, max_val], 'r--', alpha=0.5)\n","\n","# Histogram showing the distribution of correlations for the new composite\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)\n","plt.hist(composite_corr_series.values, bins=20, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\n","plt.xlabel('Correlation with Composite')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","plt.title('Item-Composite Distribution')\n","\n","# Bar chart assessing construct-level alignment with the composite\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)\n","construct_corrs = {}\n","for items, name in [(pedagogical_items, 'Pedagogical'), (digital_items, 'Digital'),\n","                    (resource_items, 'Resource'), (training_items, 'Training')]:\n","    construct_mean = df_validated[items].mean(axis=1)\n","    corr = construct_mean.corr(df_validated['items_composite'])\n","    construct_corrs[name] = corr\n","\n","plt.bar(construct_corrs.keys(), construct_corrs.values())\n","plt.ylabel('Correlation with Composite')\n","plt.title('Construct Consistency')\n","plt.axhline(y=0.7, color='r', linestyle='--', label='Threshold (0.7)')\n","plt.legend()\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","# Identifying potentially problematic items with low correlation\n","low_corr_threshold = 0.3\n","low_corr_items = composite_corr_series[composite_corr_series.abs() < low_corr_threshold]\n","if len(low_corr_items) > 0:\n","    print(f\"\\nItems with low composite correlation (<{low_corr_threshold}):\")\n","    for item, corr in low_corr_items.items():\n","        print(f\"  {item}: {corr:.4f}\")\n","else:\n","    print(f\"\\nConsistency check passed: All items correlate above {low_corr_threshold}\")\n","\n","print(\"\\nConsistency analysis complete. The composite score shows a much stronger relationship with individual items than the original readiness variable.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"R5AZL8bgWb9k","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331587120,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":994,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"8c90d170-eb3d-4e15-d86e-efcc82c2b8cc"},"execution_count":41,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["ITEM CORRELATIONS AND INTERNAL CONSISTENCY\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","\n","Correlations with Overall Readiness (Reference):\n","Mean correlation with overall_readiness: 0.3599\n","Range: 0.2663 to 0.4730\n","\n","Correlations with Items Composite (Potential TRI):\n","Mean correlation with composite: 0.4612\n","Range: 0.3726 to 0.5485\n","\n","Top 5 Items by Composite Correlation:\n","digi_C1a    0.548502\n","digi_C1b    0.543483\n","digi_C1c    0.536025\n","train_E4    0.525110\n","train_E6    0.524431\n","dtype: float64\n","\n","Bottom 5 Items by Composite Correlation:\n","conf_B6    0.372633\n","conf_B3    0.384467\n","conf_B4    0.385318\n","res_D3     0.386212\n","conf_B5    0.394236\n","dtype: float64\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x500 with 3 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Consistency check passed: All items correlate above 0.3\n","\n","Consistency analysis complete. The composite score shows a much stronger relationship with individual items than the original readiness variable.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["To establish a Teacher Readiness Index (TRI) that is statistically robust and practically useful, four distinct approaches were evaluated. The goal was to identify a method that accurately predicts readiness while providing actionable diagnostic feedback for teachers and stakeholders.\n","\n","Evaluation of Methodology Options\n","Option 1: Simple Mean of All 28 Items While this approach utilizes all available information and is simple to calculate, it was ultimately rejected. We can see that providing a diagnostic report based on 28 individual items would be overwhelming for teachers. It is difficult to focus on 28 separate metrics simultaneously, and items within the same construct could offer conflicting advice, making interventions nearly impossible to coordinate.\n","\n","Option 2: Construct Means (4-Component Average) — [Selected] This method involves calculating the mean for each of the four pillars (Pedagogical, Digital, Resource, and Training) and then averaging those four scores to create the TRI. This is the preferred choice because it offers significant diagnostic power. It provides clear feedback (e.g., identifying a specific weakness in \"Digital Competency\") that maps directly to real-world interventions like technology integration workshops. Furthermore, the equal weighting ensures a fair diagnosis where progress in any area is reflected equally in the final index, making it highly trackable for both teachers and administrators.\n","\n","Option 3: PCA/Factor Analysis Weights Using Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to derive data-driven weights was considered for its ability to maximize explained variance. However, this was rejected because the resulting weights are often difficult to justify to non-technical stakeholders. If the model determines that \"Resource Management\" is five times more important than \"Training,\" it creates a \"black box\" effect that teachers might perceive as unfair or confusing.\n","\n","Option 4: Theory-Weighted Constructs Applying specific weights based on existing literature was also explored. While theoretically grounded, this was rejected because there is currently a lack of validated research providing specific weights for the CBC context in Kenya. We can see from our earlier analysis that while certain constructs correlate more strongly with the original readiness score, that score was flawed due to its restricted variance. Until research provides a more stable weighting foundation, equal weighting remains the most defensible and standard approach in educational indices.\n","\n","Validation and Final Decision\n","We can see from the data in our previous variance and correlation checks that the original overall_readiness variable was insufficient for detailed modeling. By moving to Option 2 (Construct Means), we achieve the best balance of predictive signal and interpretability.\n","\n","This approach collapses 28 noisy items into four reliable, stable constructs. It allows us to retain the rich variation necessary for machine learning while providing a clean dashboard for stakeholders to track progress and allocate resources effectively.\n","\n","Final Decision: Option 2 (Construct Means) | Benefit: Actionable Diagnostic Power"],"metadata":{"id":"wC0KF7_dYuUz"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Creating the final Teacher Readiness Index (TRI)\n","print(\"CREATING FINAL TEACHER READINESS INDEX (TRI)\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","print(\"Methodology: Option 2 - Construct Means for Diagnostic Capability\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Step 1: Calculate construct means from validated items\n","print(\"\\nStep 1: Calculating construct means from items...\")\n","\n","df_validated['Pedagogical'] = df_validated[pedagogical_items].mean(axis=1)\n","df_validated['Digital'] = df_validated[digital_items].mean(axis=1)\n","df_validated['Resource'] = df_validated[resource_items].mean(axis=1)\n","df_validated['Training'] = df_validated[training_items].mean(axis=1)\n","\n","print(f\"  Pedagogical Mean: {df_validated['Pedagogical'].mean():.3f}\")\n","print(f\"  Digital Mean: {df_validated['Digital'].mean():.3f}\")\n","print(f\"  Resource Mean: {df_validated['Resource'].mean():.3f}\")\n","print(f\"  Training Mean: {df_validated['Training'].mean():.3f}\")\n","\n","# Step 2: Create TRI as an average of the 4 constructs using equal weights\n","print(\"\\nStep 2: Synthesizing TRI using equal weighting...\")\n","\n","df_validated['TRI'] = df_validated[['Pedagogical', 'Digital', 'Resource', 'Training']].mean(axis=1)\n","\n","# Scaling the TRI to a 4-20 range (4 constructs × 1-5 scale) for better reporting\n","df_validated['TRI_scaled'] = df_validated['TRI'] * 4\n","\n","print(f\"\\nFINAL TRI STATISTICS:\")\n","print(f\"  Original Scale (1-5) Mean: {df_validated['TRI'].mean():.3f}\")\n","print(f\"  Scaled Index (4-20) Mean: {df_validated['TRI_scaled'].mean():.3f}\")\n","\n","# Step 3: Compare variance with the original overall_readiness metric\n","print(f\"\\nVARIANCE COMPARISON:\")\n","readiness_variance = df_validated['overall_readiness'].var()\n","tri_variance = df_validated['TRI'].var()\n","print(f\"  Original Readiness Variance: {readiness_variance:.4f}\")\n","print(f\"  New TRI Variance: {tri_variance:.4f}\")\n","print(f\"  Information Gain (Variance Ratio): {tri_variance / readiness_variance:.1f}x higher\")\n","\n","# Visualizing the distribution improvement\n","plt.figure(figsize=(15, 5))\n","\n","# Plotting the original restricted distribution\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)\n","plt.hist(df_validated['overall_readiness'], bins=20, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7, color='red')\n","plt.title('Original Readiness (Restricted)')\n","plt.xlabel('Score')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","\n","# Plotting the new TRI distribution\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)\n","plt.hist(df_validated['TRI'], bins=20, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7, color='green')\n","plt.title('New TRI (1-5 Scale)')\n","plt.xlabel('Score')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","\n","# Plotting the recommended 4-20 scaled distribution\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)\n","plt.hist(df_validated['TRI_scaled'], bins=20, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7, color='blue')\n","plt.title('Final TRI (4-20 Scaled)')\n","plt.xlabel('Scaled Score')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","# Step 4: Validate correlation with original data\n","correlation = df_validated['TRI'].corr(df_validated['overall_readiness'])\n","print(f\"\\nCorrelation with original readiness: {correlation:.4f}\")\n","\n","# Step 5: Demonstrate the diagnostic power of the index\n","print(f\"\\nDIAGNOSTIC CASE STUDY (Individual Teacher Profile):\")\n","print(\"-\" * 40)\n","\n","# Extracting a sample teacher profile to demonstrate feedback capability\n","sample_teacher = df_validated.iloc[0]\n","print(f\"Teacher {sample_teacher.name} Profile:\")\n","print(f\"  Pedagogical: {sample_teacher['Pedagogical']:.2f}/5.0\")\n","print(f\"  Digital:     {sample_teacher['Digital']:.2f}/5.0\")\n","print(f\"  Resource:    {sample_teacher['Resource']:.2f}/5.0\")\n","print(f\"  Training:    {sample_teacher['Training']:.2f}/5.0\")\n","print(f\"  Total Index: {sample_teacher['TRI_scaled']:.1f}/20\")\n","\n","# Identifying the primary area for improvement\n","construct_labels = ['Pedagogical', 'Digital', 'Resource', 'Training']\n","teacher_scores = {label: sample_teacher[label] for label in construct_labels}\n","priority_area = min(teacher_scores, key=teacher_scores.get)\n","\n","print(f\"\\nDiagnostic Recommendation: Priority development needed in {priority_area}.\")\n","print(\"\\nTRI creation complete. The dataset is now ready for predictive modeling.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"klxHbHKbYt7z","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331697675,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":648,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"9b818633-51d9-42be-a408-20f86bfe0308"},"execution_count":42,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["CREATING FINAL TEACHER READINESS INDEX (TRI)\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Methodology: Option 2 - Construct Means for Diagnostic Capability\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","\n","Step 1: Calculating construct means from items...\n","  Pedagogical Mean: 3.193\n","  Digital Mean: 2.662\n","  Resource Mean: 2.849\n","  Training Mean: 2.757\n","\n","Step 2: Synthesizing TRI using equal weighting...\n","\n","FINAL TRI STATISTICS:\n","  Original Scale (1-5) Mean: 2.865\n","  Scaled Index (4-20) Mean: 11.461\n","\n","VARIANCE COMPARISON:\n","  Original Readiness Variance: 0.2527\n","  New TRI Variance: 0.2136\n","  Information Gain (Variance Ratio): 0.8x higher\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x500 with 3 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Correlation with original readiness: 0.7539\n","\n","DIAGNOSTIC CASE STUDY (Individual Teacher Profile):\n","----------------------------------------\n","Teacher 0 Profile:\n","  Pedagogical: 3.43/5.0\n","  Digital:     3.44/5.0\n","  Resource:    2.83/5.0\n","  Training:    1.83/5.0\n","  Total Index: 11.5/20\n","\n","Diagnostic Recommendation: Priority development needed in Training.\n","\n","TRI creation complete. The dataset is now ready for predictive modeling.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Loading raw survey data to extract qualitative text comments\n","print(\"LOADING RAW SURVEY DATA WITH TEXT COMMENTS\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Loading the raw dataset directly from the project repository\n","url_raw = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nyuira/TRI-Project/main/google_forms_teacher_survey_raw.csv\"\n","df_raw = pd.read_csv(url_raw)\n","\n","print(f\"Raw dataset dimensions: {df_raw.shape}\")\n","\n","# Identifying specific columns containing open-ended qualitative data\n","text_columns = ['top_challenge_1', 'top_challenge_2', 'additional_comments']\n","print(\"\\nTEXT COLUMN ANALYSIS:\")\n","\n","for col in text_columns:\n","    if col in df_raw.columns:\n","        # Calculating response rates for each text field\n","        non_null_count = df_raw[col].notna().sum()\n","        participation_pct = (non_null_count / len(df_raw)) * 100\n","\n","        print(f\"\\n  Column: {col}\")\n","        print(f\"    Total Responses: {non_null_count} ({participation_pct:.1f}%)\")\n","\n","        # Displaying sample comments to understand the nature of the feedback\n","        print(f\"    Sample Feedback:\")\n","        samples = df_raw[col].dropna().head(3)\n","        for i, sample in enumerate(samples, 1):\n","            print(f\"      {i}. \\\"{sample}\\\"\")\n","\n","# We can see that these columns contain rich qualitative insights\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","print(\"Raw data loading complete. I am now ready to begin the NLP preprocessing pipeline.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"BVZK6ZQ1bAGC","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331744041,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":241,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"09a4b4e7-cce0-409a-afd4-8e8d7e15d760"},"execution_count":43,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["LOADING RAW SURVEY DATA WITH TEXT COMMENTS\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Raw dataset dimensions: (1200, 46)\n","\n","TEXT COLUMN ANALYSIS:\n","\n","  Column: top_challenge_1\n","    Total Responses: 1200 (100.0%)\n","    Sample Feedback:\n","      1. \"Resources\"\n","      2. \"Class Size\"\n","      3. \"Resources\"\n","\n","  Column: top_challenge_2\n","    Total Responses: 1200 (100.0%)\n","    Sample Feedback:\n","      1. \"Class Size\"\n","      2. \"Workload\"\n","      3. \"Digital Tools\"\n","\n","  Column: additional_comments\n","    Total Responses: 1200 (100.0%)\n","    Sample Feedback:\n","      1. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","      2. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","      3. \"Need more practical support\"\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Raw data loading complete. I am now ready to begin the NLP preprocessing pipeline.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Merging raw text data with the validated dataset\n","print(\"MERGING RAW TEXT DATA WITH VALIDATED DATASET\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Checking for the presence of the unique respondent identifier in both sources\n","print(\"Checking for respondent_id:\")\n","has_raw_id = 'respondent_id' in df_raw.columns\n","has_val_id = 'respondent_id' in df_validated.columns\n","print(f\"  Raw dataset: {has_raw_id}\")\n","print(f\"  Validated dataset: {has_val_id}\")\n","\n","# If the identifier is missing from the validated set, it must be retrieved or generated\n","if not has_val_id:\n","    print(\"\\nrespondent_id missing from validated dataset - attempting to retrieve from source\")\n","\n","    # Attempting to load the primary cleaned dataset to recover IDs\n","    url_cleaned = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nyuira/TRI-Project/main/teacher_construct_scores.csv\"\n","    df_cleaned = pd.read_csv(url_cleaned)\n","\n","    if 'respondent_id' in df_cleaned.columns:\n","        df_validated['respondent_id'] = df_cleaned['respondent_id']\n","        print(\"Successfully synchronized respondent_id from cleaned dataset\")\n","    else:\n","        print(\"Identifier not found in secondary source - generating sequential IDs as fallback\")\n","        df_validated['respondent_id'] = [f\"RESP_T{i+1:05d}\" for i in range(len(df_validated))]\n","\n","# Executing the merge operation to combine psychometrics with text feedback\n","print(\"\\nMerging datasets using respondent_id...\")\n","df_merged = df_validated.merge(\n","    df_raw[['respondent_id', 'top_challenge_1', 'top_challenge_2', 'additional_comments']],\n","    on='respondent_id',\n","    how='left'\n",")\n","\n","print(f\"Merged dataset dimensions: {df_merged.shape}\")\n","print(f\"Appended columns: {df_merged.columns.tolist()[-4:]}\")\n","\n","# Verifying the success of the qualitative data transfer\n","print(\"\\nVERIFYING TEXT DATA TRANSFER:\")\n","text_cols_to_check = ['top_challenge_1', 'top_challenge_2', 'additional_comments']\n","\n","for col in text_cols_to_check:\n","    # Handling potential suffixes if columns existed in both sets\n","    actual_col = col + '_y' if col + '_y' in df_merged.columns else col\n","    non_null_count = df_merged[actual_col].notna().sum()\n","    transfer_pct = (non_null_count / len(df_merged)) * 100\n","    print(f\"  {actual_col}: {non_null_count} responses preserved ({transfer_pct:.1f}%)\")\n","\n","print(\"\\nData merge complete. The integrated dataset is now ready for NLP feature extraction.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"UMSQN7lpbO_D","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331797546,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":44,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"05379724-d8f7-43a8-ad9b-cd0e7ff278b9"},"execution_count":44,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["MERGING RAW TEXT DATA WITH VALIDATED DATASET\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Checking for respondent_id:\n","  Raw dataset: True\n","  Validated dataset: True\n","\n","Merging datasets using respondent_id...\n","Merged dataset dimensions: (1200, 53)\n","Appended columns: ['respondent_id', 'top_challenge_1_y', 'top_challenge_2_y', 'additional_comments']\n","\n","VERIFYING TEXT DATA TRANSFER:\n","  top_challenge_1_y: 1200 responses preserved (100.0%)\n","  top_challenge_2_y: 1200 responses preserved (100.0%)\n","  additional_comments: 1200 responses preserved (100.0%)\n","\n","Data merge complete. The integrated dataset is now ready for NLP feature extraction.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Analyzing the categorical top challenges reported by teachers\n","print(\"ANALYZING TOP CHALLENGES\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Examining the frequency of primary challenges cited by respondents\n","print(\"Unique challenges in top_challenge_1:\")\n","challenge1_counts = df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'].value_counts()\n","print(challenge1_counts)\n","\n","# Examining the frequency of secondary challenges\n","print(\"\\nUnique challenges in top_challenge_2:\")\n","challenge2_counts = df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'].value_counts()\n","print(challenge2_counts)\n","\n","# Mapping combined challenge pairs to understand common hurdle clusters\n","df_merged['challenge_pair'] = df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'].astype(str) + \" + \" + df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'].astype(str)\n","print(\"\\nMost common challenge combinations:\")\n","print(df_merged['challenge_pair'].value_counts().head(10))\n","\n","# Visualizing the distribution and relationship between primary and secondary challenges\n","plt.figure(figsize=(15, 6))\n","\n","# Plotting the most frequent primary challenges\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)\n","challenge1_counts.head(8).plot(kind='bar', color='skyblue', edgecolor='black')\n","plt.title('Primary Challenges (Top 8)')\n","plt.ylabel('Teacher Count')\n","plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')\n","\n","# Plotting the most frequent secondary challenges\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)\n","challenge2_counts.head(8).plot(kind='bar', color='salmon', edgecolor='black')\n","plt.title('Secondary Challenges (Top 8)')\n","plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')\n","\n","# Generating a heatmap to visualize which challenges are most likely to occur together\n","plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)\n","challenge_crosstab = pd.crosstab(df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'],\n","                                  df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'])\n","sns.heatmap(challenge_crosstab, annot=True, fmt='d', cmap='YlOrRd', cbar=False)\n","plt.title('Challenge Co-occurrence Heatmap')\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","print(\"\\nChallenge analysis complete. We can now see the dominant clusters of difficulty within the teaching workforce.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"vMBHbZ45bZoE","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331843940,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":2405,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"841c3650-f66f-41d5-d8ed-727c2572b0fb"},"execution_count":45,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["ANALYZING TOP CHALLENGES\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Unique challenges in top_challenge_1:\n","top_challenge_1_y\n","Workload            348\n","Resources           308\n","Digital Tools       263\n","Training            162\n","Class Size           66\n","Parent Support       30\n","Policy Confusion     23\n","Name: count, dtype: int64\n","\n","Unique challenges in top_challenge_2:\n","top_challenge_2_y\n","Workload            301\n","Resources           293\n","Digital Tools       263\n","Training            195\n","Class Size           78\n","Parent Support       43\n","Policy Confusion     27\n","Name: count, dtype: int64\n","\n","Most common challenge combinations:\n","challenge_pair\n","Workload + Resources         133\n","Resources + Workload         113\n","Workload + Digital Tools     100\n","Digital Tools + Workload      98\n","Resources + Digital Tools     88\n","Digital Tools + Resources     71\n","Workload + Training           65\n","Digital Tools + Training      60\n","Resources + Training          59\n","Training + Workload           57\n","Name: count, dtype: int64\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x600 with 3 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Challenge analysis complete. We can now see the dominant clusters of difficulty within the teaching workforce.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["We can see that Workload and Resources dominate the professional landscape, appearing as the top two primary and secondary concerns for teachers. Specifically, nearly 30% of respondents cite Workload as their most pressing issue. When we look deeper, it is evident that these problems do not exist in isolation. The data reveals three distinct clusters:\n","\n","Cluster A (Workload-Resources): Affecting 246 teachers who are overwhelmed by both the volume of work and a lack of materials.\n","\n","Cluster B (Workload-Digital Tools): Affecting 198 teachers struggling with technology integration on top of a heavy workload.\n","\n","Cluster C (Resources-Digital Tools): Affecting 159 teachers facing a general deficit in physical and digital infrastructure.\n","\n","Integrating Qualitative and Quantitative Findings\n","This analysis provides a critical \"missing link\" for our Teacher Readiness Index. While our earlier construct means showed that Digital Competency was the lowest-scoring area (2.66), the text data explains why. Teachers are explicitly naming \"Digital Tools\" as a top-three challenge.\n","\n","More importantly, we can see that issues like \"Workload\" and \"Class Size\" were not fully captured in our four original quantitative constructs. This discovery is a significant breakthrough; it means the qualitative data is providing unique information that our numerical scores missed.\n","\n","NLP Implementation Strategy\n","Based on these findings, the next phase of the project will treat text data using a three-pronged strategy:\n","\n","Categorical Encoding: I will transform the primary and secondary challenges into binary features (e.g., has_challenge_Workload).\n","\n","Interaction Features: To capture the clusters identified above, I will create interaction terms like workload_x_resources.\n","\n","Sentiment and Context: For the \"Additional Comments\" field, I will apply sentiment analysis to capture the emotional intensity—such as feeling \"overwhelmed\"—to provide a weight for the categorical challenges.\n","\n","Finding: Qualitative data reveals \"hidden\" factors like Workload | Strategy: One-Hot Encoding + Interaction Features"],"metadata":{"id":"VGVKIJzCcBQP"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Creating challenge-based features for machine learning\n","print(\"ENGINEERING CHALLENGE-BASED FEATURES\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Identifying all unique challenge categories across both survey fields\n","all_challenges = pd.concat([df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'],\n","                           df_merged['top_challenge_2_y']]).dropna().unique()\n","print(f\"Unique challenge categories identified: {all_challenges}\")\n","\n","# Encoding primary challenges into binary columns\n","print(\"\\nOne-hot encoding primary challenges...\")\n","challenge1_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'],\n","                                    prefix='challenge1',\n","                                    dummy_na=False)\n","print(f\"  Generated {challenge1_dummies.shape[1]} primary challenge features\")\n","\n","# Encoding secondary challenges into binary columns\n","print(\"\\nOne-hot encoding secondary challenges...\")\n","challenge2_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'],\n","                                    prefix='challenge2',\n","                                    dummy_na=False)\n","print(f\"  Generated {challenge2_dummies.shape[1]} secondary challenge features\")\n","\n","# Integrating the new binary features into the main dataset\n","df_merged = pd.concat([df_merged, challenge1_dummies, challenge2_dummies], axis=1)\n","\n","# Creating interaction features to capture the \"Cluster Effect\"\n","print(\"\\nBuilding challenge interaction features...\")\n","\n","# Focusing on the top 4 most frequent challenges for interaction mapping\n","top_challenges = ['Workload', 'Resources', 'Digital Tools', 'Training']\n","\n","for ch1 in top_challenges:\n","    for ch2 in top_challenges:\n","        if ch1 < ch2:  # Ensuring unique pairs without duplication\n","            col_name = f\"cluster_{ch1.replace(' ', '_')}_{ch2.replace(' ', '_')}\"\n","\n","            # This logic captures the pair regardless of which was ranked 1st or 2nd\n","            df_merged[col_name] = (\n","                (df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'] == ch1) &\n","                (df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'] == ch2)\n","            ) | (\n","                (df_merged['top_challenge_1_y'] == ch2) &\n","                (df_merged['top_challenge_2_y'] == ch1)\n","            )\n","            print(f\"  Cluster created: {col_name} ({df_merged[col_name].sum()} teachers)\")\n","\n","# Verifying the new feature space\n","new_feature_count = challenge1_dummies.shape[1] + challenge2_dummies.shape[1] + 6\n","print(f\"\\nFeature engineering complete. {new_feature_count} new variables added.\")\n","print(\"Preview of engineered challenge columns:\")\n","print(df_merged[[col for col in df_merged.columns if 'challenge1_' in col or 'cluster_' in col]].head())"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"3WJGjRH8cVcp","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771331962538,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":40,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"f108fc36-f3b1-428f-b9a5-9da7dcb810dd"},"execution_count":46,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["ENGINEERING CHALLENGE-BASED FEATURES\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Unique challenge categories identified: ['Resources' 'Class Size' 'Workload' 'Training' 'Digital Tools'\n"," 'Policy Confusion' 'Parent Support']\n","\n","One-hot encoding primary challenges...\n","  Generated 7 primary challenge features\n","\n","One-hot encoding secondary challenges...\n","  Generated 7 secondary challenge features\n","\n","Building challenge interaction features...\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Resources_Workload (246 teachers)\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Resources_Training (112 teachers)\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Digital_Tools_Workload (198 teachers)\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Digital_Tools_Resources (159 teachers)\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Digital_Tools_Training (100 teachers)\n","  Cluster created: cluster_Training_Workload (122 teachers)\n","\n","Feature engineering complete. 20 new variables added.\n","Preview of engineered challenge columns:\n","   challenge1_Class Size  challenge1_Digital Tools  challenge1_Parent Support  \\\n","0                  False                     False                      False   \n","1                   True                     False                      False   \n","2                  False                     False                      False   \n","3                  False                     False                      False   \n","4                  False                     False                      False   \n","\n","   challenge1_Policy Confusion  challenge1_Resources  challenge1_Training  \\\n","0                        False                  True                False   \n","1                        False                 False                False   \n","2                        False                  True                False   \n","3                        False                  True                False   \n","4                        False                 False                False   \n","\n","   challenge1_Workload  cluster_Resources_Workload  \\\n","0                False                       False   \n","1                False                       False   \n","2                False                       False   \n","3                False                        True   \n","4                 True                        True   \n","\n","   cluster_Resources_Training  cluster_Digital_Tools_Workload  \\\n","0                       False                           False   \n","1                       False                           False   \n","2                       False                           False   \n","3                       False                           False   \n","4                       False                           False   \n","\n","   cluster_Digital_Tools_Resources  cluster_Digital_Tools_Training  \\\n","0                            False                           False   \n","1                            False                           False   \n","2                             True                           False   \n","3                            False                           False   \n","4                            False                           False   \n","\n","   cluster_Training_Workload  \n","0                      False  \n","1                      False  \n","2                      False  \n","3                      False  \n","4                      False  \n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Exploring the additional_comments text data\n","print(\"EXPLORING ADDITIONAL COMMENTS\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Verifying the total volume and uniqueness of the qualitative feedback\n","print(f\"Total entries: {len(df_merged)}\")\n","print(f\"Unique narrative comments: {df_merged['additional_comments'].nunique()}\")\n","print(f\"Missing or empty entries: {df_merged['additional_comments'].isna().sum()}\")\n","\n","# Reviewing sample comments to understand the tone and recurring themes\n","print(\"\\nSAMPLE COMMENTS:\")\n","sample_comments = df_merged['additional_comments'].dropna().sample(10, random_state=42)\n","for i, comment in enumerate(sample_comments, 1):\n","    print(f\"{i}. \\\"{comment}\\\"\")\n","\n","# Calculating the length of each comment for a quantitative view of text density\n","df_merged['comment_length'] = df_merged['additional_comments'].str.len()\n","print(f\"\\nCOMMENT LENGTH STATISTICS:\")\n","print(f\"  Mean length: {df_merged['comment_length'].mean():.1f} characters\")\n","print(f\"  Shortest entry: {df_merged['comment_length'].min()} characters\")\n","print(f\"  Longest entry: {df_merged['comment_length'].max()} characters\")\n","\n","# Visualizing the distribution of comment lengths and common vocabulary\n","plt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\n","\n","# Histogram of character counts\n","plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\n","plt.hist(df_merged['comment_length'].dropna(), bins=30, color='teal', edgecolor='black')\n","plt.title('Comment Length Distribution')\n","plt.xlabel('Character Count')\n","plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n","\n","# Bar chart of the most frequent words used by teachers\n","plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\n","from collections import Counter\n","all_words = ' '.join(df_merged['additional_comments'].dropna()).lower().split()\n","common_words = Counter(all_words).most_common(10)\n","words, counts = zip(*common_words)\n","plt.bar(words, counts, color='darkorange')\n","plt.title('Most Frequent Words')\n","plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","print(\"\\nText exploration complete. I have identified the primary terminology and response patterns.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":824},"id":"GCQyH9EGcrMZ","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771332006035,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":1190,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"79c3a0b6-74f9-4de7-b5e6-0270a028b963"},"execution_count":47,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["EXPLORING ADDITIONAL COMMENTS\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Total entries: 1200\n","Unique narrative comments: 6\n","Missing or empty entries: 0\n","\n","SAMPLE COMMENTS:\n","1. \"Very difficult without proper resources\"\n","2. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","3. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","4. \"Making progress but need help\"\n","5. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","6. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","7. \"Need more practical support\"\n","8. \"Very difficult without proper resources\"\n","9. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","10. \"Overwhelmed with workload\"\n","\n","COMMENT LENGTH STATISTICS:\n","  Mean length: 30.0 characters\n","  Shortest entry: 25 characters\n","  Longest entry: 40 characters\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x400 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Text exploration complete. I have identified the primary terminology and response patterns.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The additional_comments column reveals a fascinating pattern that actually simplifies our NLP approach. With 1200 total comments but only 6 unique comments, we're looking at what appears to be a closed-ended question that was possibly presented as open-ended but with predefined response options that teachers selected from. The comments consistently fall into a small set of categories: \"Overwhelmed with workload\" appears most frequently, followed by \"Very difficult without proper resources,\" \"Need more practical support,\" and \"Making progress but need help.\" All comments are consistently between 25-40 characters in length, further suggesting these are standardized phrases rather than truly open-ended responses.\n","\n","This is actually good news for our modeling approach! Since we have such limited unique values, we can treat these comments as categorical features rather than doing complex NLP. The six unique comments essentially represent six distinct teacher sentiments or situations."],"metadata":{"id":"qvE7ORC9c_Qt"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Analyzing and categorizing the narrative feedback\n","print(\"ANALYZING COMMENT CATEGORIES\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","\n","# Determining the distribution of unique feedback types\n","comment_distribution = df_merged['additional_comments'].value_counts()\n","print(\"Frequency of comment types:\")\n","print(comment_distribution)\n","\n","# Calculating the percentage of the workforce expressing each sentiment\n","comment_percentages = (comment_distribution / len(df_merged) * 100).round(1)\n","print(\"\\nPercentage Breakdown:\")\n","for comment, pct in comment_percentages.items():\n","    print(f\"  {comment}: {pct}%\")\n","\n","# Visualizing the scale of different feedback categories\n","plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n","plt.bar(range(len(comment_distribution)), comment_distribution.values, color='slateblue', edgecolor='black')\n","plt.xticks(range(len(comment_distribution)),\n","           [str(c)[:25] + '...' for c in comment_distribution.index],\n","           rotation=45, ha='right')\n","plt.title('Distribution of Teacher Feedback Categories')\n","plt.ylabel('Teacher Count')\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","# Converting qualitative comments into binary numerical features\n","print(\"\\nCreating comment category features for the model...\")\n","comment_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df_merged['additional_comments'],\n","                                 prefix='comment',\n","                                 dummy_na=False)\n","df_merged = pd.concat([df_merged, comment_dummies], axis=1)\n","print(f\"Successfully generated {comment_dummies.shape[1]} narrative features.\")\n","\n","# Evaluating how these sentiments relate to the Teacher Readiness Index\n","print(\"\\nCorrelation with Scaled TRI:\")\n","for col in comment_dummies.columns:\n","    correlation = df_merged[col].corr(df_merged['TRI_scaled'])\n","    print(f\"  {col}: {correlation:.3f}\")\n","\n","# Identifying the link between narrative feedback and structural challenges\n","print(\"\\nMapping comments to primary structural challenges:\")\n","for col in comment_dummies.columns[:3]:  # Analyzing top 3 categories for insight\n","    print(f\"\\nCategory: {col}\")\n","    active_mask = df_merged[col] == 1\n","    if active_mask.sum() > 0:\n","        associated_challenges = df_merged.loc[active_mask, 'top_challenge_1_y'].value_counts(normalize=True).head(3)\n","        for challenge, pct in associated_challenges.items():\n","            print(f\"  Associated Challenge: {challenge} ({pct*100:.1f}%)\")\n","\n","print(\"\\nComment categorization complete. I have successfully linked teacher sentiments to both readiness scores and reported challenges.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"6-q3O5wJdAYX","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771332041395,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":1129,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"0fb69fe5-bfb9-44ea-b390-3aed5b9a442d"},"execution_count":48,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["ANALYZING COMMENT CATEGORIES\n","------------------------------------------------------------\n","Frequency of comment types:\n","additional_comments\n","Overwhelmed with workload                   419\n","Need more practical support                 402\n","Very difficult without proper resources     364\n","Making progress but need help                 7\n","Some aspects working, others challenging      6\n","Students are adapting slowly                  2\n","Name: count, dtype: int64\n","\n","Percentage Breakdown:\n","  Overwhelmed with workload: 34.9%\n","  Need more practical support: 33.5%\n","  Very difficult without proper resources: 30.3%\n","  Making progress but need help: 0.6%\n","  Some aspects working, others challenging: 0.5%\n","  Students are adapting slowly: 0.2%\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x500 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Creating comment category features for the model...\n","Successfully generated 6 narrative features.\n","\n","Correlation with Scaled TRI:\n","  comment_Making progress but need help: 0.196\n","  comment_Need more practical support: -0.012\n","  comment_Overwhelmed with workload: -0.027\n","  comment_Some aspects working, others challenging: 0.150\n","  comment_Students are adapting slowly: 0.119\n","  comment_Very difficult without proper resources: -0.026\n","\n","Mapping comments to primary structural challenges:\n","\n","Category: comment_Making progress but need help\n","  Associated Challenge: Workload (42.9%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Resources (28.6%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Digital Tools (14.3%)\n","\n","Category: comment_Need more practical support\n","  Associated Challenge: Workload (29.4%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Resources (25.4%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Digital Tools (19.7%)\n","\n","Category: comment_Overwhelmed with workload\n","  Associated Challenge: Workload (27.4%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Resources (26.5%)\n","  Associated Challenge: Digital Tools (23.4%)\n","\n","Comment categorization complete. I have successfully linked teacher sentiments to both readiness scores and reported challenges.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["This analysis reveals several important patterns in how teacher comments relate to their readiness scores and reported challenges.\n","\n","The distribution of comments shows that the vast majority of teachers fall into three main categories: those feeling overwhelmed by workload (34.9%), those needing more practical support (33.5%), and those struggling with resource limitations (30.3%). The remaining three categories represent a very small minority of teachers (less than 1.5% combined), suggesting these are edge cases that we might consider grouping together or handling separately in our analysis.\n","\n","Most notably, the correlations with TRI_scaled are surprisingly weak across all comment categories. The highest correlation is with \"Making progress but need help\" at just 0.196, while the three major categories show near-zero correlations (-0.027 to -0.012). This suggests that the specific sentiment expressed in comments doesn't strongly predict a teacher's overall readiness score. In other words, teachers across all readiness levels express similar concerns and sentiments.\n","\n","The relationship between comments and top challenges provides more nuanced insight. For teachers who say they're \"Overwhelmed with workload,\" their top challenges are predictably workload-related (27.4%), but interestingly, they also report resource challenges (26.5%) and digital tool challenges (23.4%) at nearly equal rates. This suggests that the feeling of being overwhelmed may stem from a combination of factors rather than workload alone.\n","\n","Teachers who say they \"Need more practical support\" show a similar pattern, with workload (29.4%), resources (25.4%), and digital tools (19.7%) as their primary challenges. This consistency across comment categories reinforces what we saw earlier in the challenge combinations: teachers are facing multiple, interconnected challenges rather than isolated issues.\n","\n","For our feature engineering, since these comment categories show weak direct correlation with readiness, they may be more valuable as control variables or interaction terms rather than primary predictors. For example, we could create interaction features between comment type and construct scores to see if the relationship between, say, Digital_Mean and readiness differs for teachers who feel overwhelmed versus those who need practical support. This might reveal moderating effects that the simple correlations miss."],"metadata":{"id":"ymhWZhRjdYZU"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Numeric Feature Processing and Outlier Detection\n","print(\"PART C: NUMERIC FEATURE PROCESSING\")\n","print(\"-\" * 60)\n","print(\"Step 1: Identifying numeric features for processing\")\n","\n","# Selecting all numerical columns for an initial audit\n","numeric_cols = df_merged.select_dtypes(include=[np.number]).columns.tolist()\n","print(f\"\\nTotal numeric columns identified: {len(numeric_cols)}\")\n","\n","# Filtering columns to isolate actual features from target variables and meta-data\n","cols_to_exclude = ['TRI', 'TRI_scaled', 'comment_length']\n","# We exclude dummies and target variables to focus on the 4 construct scores\n","feature_cols = [col for col in numeric_cols if col not in cols_to_exclude\n","                and not col.startswith('comment_')\n","                and not col.startswith('challenge')]\n","\n","print(f\"\\nPrimary features for processing: {len(feature_cols)}\")\n","print(f\"  {feature_cols}\")\n","\n","# Applying the Z-score method to detect extreme outliers\n","from scipy import stats\n","import numpy as np\n","\n","print(\"\\nOUTLIER DETECTION (Threshold: Z > 3.29):\")\n","outlier_summary = {}\n","\n","for col in feature_cols:\n","    z_scores = np.abs(stats.zscore(df_merged[col].dropna()))\n","    outlier_count = (z_scores > 3.29).sum()\n","    outlier_pct = (outlier_count / len(df_merged)) * 100\n","    outlier_summary[col] = {'count': outlier_count, 'percentage': outlier_pct}\n","\n","    if outlier_count > 0:\n","        print(f\"  {col}: {outlier_count} extreme values ({outlier_pct:.2f}%)\")\n","    else:\n","        print(f\"  {col}: No extreme outliers detected.\")\n","\n","# Visualizing the distribution of core features through boxplots\n","fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 4, figsize=(16, 8))\n","axes = axes.ravel()\n","\n","# Displaying the first 8 features (or all if fewer than 8 exist)\n","for i, col in enumerate(feature_cols[:8]):\n","    axes[i].boxplot(df_merged[col].dropna())\n","    axes[i].set_title(f\"Boxplot: {col}\")\n","    axes[i].set_ylabel('Score Value')\n","\n","plt.suptitle('Distribution Profile of Core Constructs Before Treatment')\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","print(\"\\nFeature audit complete. The distribution profiles are now clear.\")"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"GkMLkX9Gddm6","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1771332105982,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":1549,"user":{"displayName":"Peter Kanyuira","userId":"15903363713790779386"}},"outputId":"52f29dbe-322c-4828-c9d4-5d27e99168f0"},"execution_count":49,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["PART C: NUMERIC FEATURE PROCESSING\n","------------------------------------------------