core.display.Javascript object>"],"application/javascript":["download(\"download_7fc8ae4e-d6a7-4f2d-ac3c-7d08c4369eaf\", \"teacher_readiness_test.csv\", 18920)"]},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#FINAL OBSERVATIONS:\n","The feature engineering process has successfully transformed raw survey data into a robust, prediction-ready dataset with strong diagnostic capabilities. Beginning with 1200 teacher responses across 28 individual items spanning four theoretical constructs (Pedagogical, Digital, Resource, and Training), we discovered that the provided overall_readiness variable was severely limited with 75% of teachers scoring exactly 3, making it unsuitable as a target variable. This led us to create our own Teacher Readiness Index (TRI) by averaging the four construct means, an approach specifically chosen over alternatives because it maintains diagnostic capability - we can now identify which specific construct needs improvement for each teacher.\n","\n","The text data proved invaluable, revealing that Workload (29%) and Resources (26%) are the most pressing challenges, with three major challenge clusters emerging: Workload-Resources (246 teachers), Workload-Digital Tools (198 teachers), and Resources-Digital Tools (159 teachers). These qualitative insights explain aspects of teacher experience that our quantitative constructs don't capture. Interestingly, the additional comments showed only six unique responses, suggesting they were likely predefined options, with 35% of teachers reporting feeling \"Overwhelmed with workload.\"\n","\n","A critical finding was the independence of our four constructs (near-zero correlations), confirming that each measures a distinct aspect of teacher capability while all contributing meaningfully to overall readiness. The item-level analysis showed 2.5 to 6 times more variation than the original readiness measure, justifying our decision to create a new TRI. The final TRI shows excellent properties: mean of 11.46 on a 4-20 scale, standard deviation of 1.85, and strong correlation with the original measure (r=0.75) while providing much richer variation for modeling.\n","\n","Feature selection through variance threshold, correlation analysis, mutual information, and random forest importance identified 25 powerful predictors. Notably, no demographic or challenge features made the final cut - the individual items and constructs themselves are far more predictive. Training items dominate the top features, with all six training items and training_quality_mean ranking in the top 10, confirming that training quality is a primary driver of teacher readiness. The Digital_x_Training interaction also made the final set, validating our theoretical approach.\n","\n","The final dataset is properly split with school grouping, ensuring 1103 teachers from 9 schools in training and 97 teachers from 3 schools in testing, with zero school overlap to prevent data leakage. Target distributions are similar across both sets (training mean 11.44, test mean 11.70), confirming a representative split.\n","\n","This feature engineering has achieved its dual goals: creating a powerful predictive model input while maintaining diagnostic interpretability. When a teacher's readiness is predicted, we can trace back to their construct scores and identify specific areas for improvement - whether they need more training, better resources, digital skills development, or pedagogical support. The tool is now ready for modeling, where we can build algorithms to predict readiness and provide actionable recommendations for teacher development."],"metadata":{"id":"L95a7eyZq8Wz"}}]}